# screenlock-pin's two lines for /etc/pam.d/kde, shown around the stock "auth substack password-auth"
# line they go just above and just below; the stock line itself stays as it is. Only
# packaging/integrate-into-pam.sh add puts them in (just integrate-into-pam), after a diff and a
# question; install.sh never touches /etc/pam.d.
#
# crates/pam/tests/stack.rs builds its PAM stack from this very file, swapping only the module's
# path and the stock substack, so the control columns tested are the ones that would ship. The
# container test (just podman) puts them into a stock Fedora for real, through the same script.
#
#   check         the PIN: a match ends the stack with success, never sooner than the config's
#                 min_milliseconds_before_pin_unlock after the PIN checker started (kscreenlocker 6.8
#                 takes a faster answer for a broken authenticator); anything else is ignored at
#                 once, and the stock stack checks the same input as the password, faillock and all
#   after-password-auth
#                 forgives the user's own recent failures, the password among them, and unpauses
#                 the PIN, after a password unlock: libpam replays pam_setcred down to this line
#                 only after the stock password-auth stack succeeded, never after a PIN unlock or a
#                 failure. That is usually the password, but anything password-auth accepts
#                 counts, a FIDO key with authselect's with-pam-u2f too. It never reads the password
auth        sufficient    /usr/local/lib64/security/pam_screenlock_pin.so check
auth        substack      password-auth
auth        optional      /usr/local/lib64/security/pam_screenlock_pin.so after-password-auth
